# MCP Specification Conformity & OpenWebUI Compatibility

This rule ensures all development strictly adheres to the official Model Context Protocol (MCP) specification from https://modelcontextprotocol.io and maintains full compatibility with OpenWebUI as an MCP client.

## Core Principles

### 1. ALWAYS Follow MCP Specification

All implementations MUST conform to the official MCP specification:
- **Source of Truth**: https://modelcontextprotocol.io
- **SDK Reference**: @modelcontextprotocol/sdk (latest stable version)
- **Protocol Version**: Use the latest stable protocol version

### 2. OpenWebUI Compatibility Requirements

OpenWebUI functions as an MCP client and requires:
- Standard MCP transport protocols (stdio, HTTP, SSE, Streamable HTTP)
- JSON-RPC 2.0 message format
- Proper capability negotiation
- Standard tool/resource/prompt definitions

## MCP Architecture Overview

```
┌─────────────────────┐         ┌─────────────────────┐
│    MCP CLIENT       │         │    MCP SERVER       │
│  (OpenWebUI,        │◄───────►│  (This Server)      │
│   Cursor, Claude)   │ JSON-RPC│                     │
└─────────────────────┘         └─────────────────────┘
```

## Transport Layer Requirements

### Supported Transports (OpenWebUI Compatible)

| Transport | Use Case | OpenWebUI Support |
|-----------|----------|-------------------|
| stdio | Local processes | ✅ Supported |
| HTTP+SSE | Remote servers (legacy) | ✅ Supported |
| Streamable HTTP | Remote servers (recommended) | ✅ Supported |

### Transport Configuration

```typescript
// Streamable HTTP Transport (Recommended for OpenWebUI)
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "ms365": {
      "url": "https://your-server.com/mcp",
      "transportType": "streamable-http"
    }
  }
}

// SSE Transport (Legacy, still supported)
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "ms365": {
      "serverUrl": "https://your-server.com/sse",
      "transportType": "sse"
    }
  }
}
```

## Tool Definition Standards

### MUST Follow MCP Tool Schema

Every tool MUST be defined according to the MCP specification:

```typescript
interface McpToolDefinition {
  name: string;                    // Unique tool identifier (kebab-case)
  description: string;             // Clear, concise description
  inputSchema: {                   // JSON Schema for parameters
    type: 'object';
    properties: Record<string, JsonSchemaProperty>;
    required?: string[];
  };
}
```

### Tool Naming Conventions

```typescript
// ✅ CORRECT: Use descriptive, action-oriented names
'list-calendar-events'
'get-mail-message'
'send-mail'
'create-todo-task'

// ❌ WRONG: Avoid vague or non-descriptive names
'getStuff'
'doAction'
'handler1'
```

### Tool Response Format

Tools MUST return responses in standard MCP format:

```typescript
interface McpToolResult {
  content: Array<{
    type: 'text' | 'image' | 'resource';
    text?: string;
    data?: string;      // Base64 for images
    mimeType?: string;
    uri?: string;       // For resource references
  }>;
  isError?: boolean;
}
```

## Resource Definition Standards

### Resource URI Format

```typescript
// Standard resource URI patterns
'ms365://users/{userId}'
'ms365://mail/{messageId}'
'ms365://calendar/{eventId}'
'ms365://files/{driveId}/{itemId}'
```

### Resource Template Definition

```typescript
interface McpResourceTemplate {
  uriTemplate: string;           // URI template with placeholders
  name: string;                  // Human-readable name
  description?: string;          // Optional description
  mimeType?: string;             // Content MIME type
}
```

## Capability Negotiation

### Server Capabilities (MUST Declare)

```typescript
const serverCapabilities: ServerCapabilities = {
  tools: {
    listChanged: true,           // Tool list can change
  },
  resources: {
    subscribe: false,            // Resource subscriptions
    listChanged: true,           // Resource list can change
  },
  prompts: {
    listChanged: false,          // Prompts are static
  },
  logging: {},                   // Logging support
};
```

### Client Compatibility Check

```typescript
// Always check client capabilities before using features
if (clientCapabilities?.sampling) {
  // Client supports sampling
}

if (clientCapabilities?.roots?.listChanged) {
  // Client can handle root changes
}
```

## JSON-RPC 2.0 Message Format

### Request Format (OpenWebUI Sends)

```typescript
interface JsonRpcRequest {
  jsonrpc: '2.0';
  id: string | number;
  method: string;
  params?: object;
}
```

### Response Format (Server Returns)

```typescript
// Success response
interface JsonRpcSuccessResponse {
  jsonrpc: '2.0';
  id: string | number;
  result: unknown;
}

// Error response
interface JsonRpcErrorResponse {
  jsonrpc: '2.0';
  id: string | number;
  error: {
    code: number;
    message: string;
    data?: unknown;
  };
}
```

### Standard Error Codes

```typescript
const MCP_ERROR_CODES = {
  PARSE_ERROR: -32700,
  INVALID_REQUEST: -32600,
  METHOD_NOT_FOUND: -32601,
  INVALID_PARAMS: -32602,
  INTERNAL_ERROR: -32603,
};
```

## OpenWebUI-Specific Considerations

### 1. Tool Descriptions for AI

Tool descriptions MUST be clear and actionable for AI models:

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD: Clear, specific description
{
  name: 'list-mail-messages',
  description: 'List email messages from the user\'s mailbox. ' +
               'Supports filtering by folder, search query, and pagination. ' +
               'Returns message previews including subject, sender, and date.',
}

// ❌ BAD: Vague description
{
  name: 'get-emails',
  description: 'Gets emails',
}
```

### 2. Parameter Documentation

Always include detailed parameter descriptions:

```typescript
inputSchema: {
  type: 'object',
  properties: {
    folderId: {
      type: 'string',
      description: 'The mail folder ID. Use "inbox", "drafts", "sentitems", ' +
                   'or a specific folder ID. Defaults to inbox if not specified.',
    },
    top: {
      type: 'number',
      description: 'Maximum number of messages to return (1-100). Default: 10.',
      minimum: 1,
      maximum: 100,
    },
    search: {
      type: 'string',
      description: 'Search query using KQL syntax. Example: "from:john@example.com"',
    },
  },
  required: [],
}
```

### 3. Response Size Limits

OpenWebUI may have context limits. Implement pagination and truncation:

```typescript
// Limit response sizes
const MAX_ITEMS_PER_REQUEST = 50;
const MAX_CONTENT_LENGTH = 100000; // characters

// Truncate large responses with indicator
if (content.length > MAX_CONTENT_LENGTH) {
  content = content.slice(0, MAX_CONTENT_LENGTH) + '\n... [truncated]';
}
```

### 4. Error Messages for AI Understanding

Errors should be informative and actionable:

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD: Actionable error message
throw new Error(
  'Authentication required. The user must log in first using the "login" tool ' +
  'before accessing calendar events.'
);

// ❌ BAD: Technical error only
throw new Error('401 Unauthorized');
```

## Implementation Checklist

### Server Initialization

- [ ] Implement `initialize` handler with proper capability negotiation
- [ ] Return server info (name, version)
- [ ] Declare all supported capabilities
- [ ] Handle `initialized` notification

### Tool Implementation

- [ ] All tools have unique, descriptive names
- [ ] All tools have complete inputSchema with JSON Schema
- [ ] All tools return proper MCP content format
- [ ] Error handling returns isError: true with helpful messages

### Resource Implementation

- [ ] Resources use proper URI templates
- [ ] Resources declare correct MIME types
- [ ] Resource content is properly encoded

### Transport Layer

- [ ] Support Streamable HTTP for OpenWebUI
- [ ] Implement proper SSE for legacy clients
- [ ] Handle connection lifecycle correctly
- [ ] Implement proper keep-alive for long connections

## Testing OpenWebUI Compatibility

### Manual Testing Checklist

1. **Connection Test**
   ```bash
   # Test server endpoint
   curl -X POST https://your-server.com/mcp \
     -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
     -d '{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"method":"initialize","params":{...}}'
   ```

2. **Tool Discovery**
   - Verify OpenWebUI can discover all tools
   - Check tool descriptions display correctly
   - Validate parameter schemas are parsed

3. **Tool Execution**
   - Test each tool category
   - Verify response format is correct
   - Check error handling works

### Integration Test Example

```typescript
describe('OpenWebUI Compatibility', () => {
  it('should respond to initialize request', async () => {
    const response = await sendJsonRpc({
      method: 'initialize',
      params: {
        protocolVersion: '2024-11-05',
        clientInfo: { name: 'OpenWebUI', version: '1.0.0' },
        capabilities: {},
      },
    });
    
    expect(response.result.serverInfo).toBeDefined();
    expect(response.result.capabilities.tools).toBeDefined();
  });

  it('should list tools with proper schema', async () => {
    const response = await sendJsonRpc({ method: 'tools/list' });
    
    for (const tool of response.result.tools) {
      expect(tool.name).toMatch(/^[a-z][a-z0-9-]*$/);
      expect(tool.description).toBeTruthy();
      expect(tool.inputSchema.type).toBe('object');
    }
  });
});
```

## Version Compatibility

### Protocol Version Support

```typescript
const SUPPORTED_PROTOCOL_VERSIONS = [
  '2024-11-05',  // Current stable
  '2025-03-26',  // Latest with auth
];

// Negotiate version during initialize
function negotiateVersion(clientVersion: string): string {
  if (SUPPORTED_PROTOCOL_VERSIONS.includes(clientVersion)) {
    return clientVersion;
  }
  return SUPPORTED_PROTOCOL_VERSIONS[0]; // Fallback to stable
}
```

